],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignee":null,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2022-01-07T23:14:15Z","updated_at":"2022-01-08T10:30:59Z","closed_at":"2022-01-08T10:30:59Z","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Neovim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.6.1 release\n\n### Language server name/version\n\nnot server related\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nLinux arch 5.15.13-arch1-1\n\n### Steps to reproduce using \"nvim -u minimal_init.lua\"\n\n1. Edit some new file `nvim test.txt`.\r\n2. Insert some text, and write `:w` the buffer.\r\n3. Now open <kbd>o</kbd> a new line and write some more text, *do not write/save yet*.\r\n4. Make a visual selection in the new line and execute `:'<,'>lua print(vim.inspect(vim.lsp.util.make_given_range_params()))`. You'll see that the start col will be `0` and the end col will be `1`, regardless of the selection.\r\n5. If you now save the buffer `:w` and repeat the previous step, you'll see that the start and end cols will have the correct values that correspond to your selection.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nWhen opening a new line and not saving the buffer first, the returned range should be correct (match the selection).\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nWhen opening a new line and not saving the buffer first, the returned range will always have the start col set to `0` and the end col set to `1`, no matter the selection.\n\n### Log file\n\n_No response_","closed_by":{"login":"mfussenegger","id":38700,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM4NzAw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/38700?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mfussenegger","html_url":"https://github.com/mfussenegger","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mfussenegger/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com